 ### [Volume Level-of-Detail (LOD) Rendering](4614-volume-level-of-detail-lod-rendering.md)
-Volume Level-of-Detail (LOD) Rendering is a method that maximizes image quality within user-specified frame rate and system constraints (RAM, texture memory, bandwidth) by adaptively modifying the set of rendered cells. It differs from static LOD methods by maintaining an "active cut" of octree nodes that covers the model space exactly once, dynamically splitting or merging nodes to balance visual error against performance. Unlike sibling methods that may rely on global resolution factors or fixed hierarchies, this approach uses a queue-based error metric to drive local refinement or coarsening in real-time.
+Volume Level-of-Detail (LOD) Rendering is a method that maximizes image quality within user-specified frame rate and system constraints (RAM, texture memory, bandwidth) by adaptively modifying the set of rendered octree cells. It solves the problem of maintaining interactivity for large datasets by dynamically adjusting the resolution of the volume representation based on viewing parameters and hardware limits. Unlike static LOD methods that precompute fixed levels, this approach maintains an "active cut" of the octree, splitting or merging nodes every frame to balance visual fidelity against rendering cost. It differs from sibling methods like [Dynamic Volume Resolution & Alpha Mask Update](2539-dynamic-volume-resolution-alpha-mask-update.md) by explicitly managing a hierarchical cut of non-leaf nodes rather than just updating alpha masks or global resolution scales. See [Volume Level-of-Detail (LOD) Rendering](4614-volume-level-of-detail-lod-rendering.md).
 
 ### [Focus and Context Multiresolution Rendering](8163-focus-and-context-multiresolution-rendering.md)
-Focus and Context Multiresolution Rendering is a technique that balances processing capacity between a high-detail focus region and a lower-detail context region to improve interactivity in time-critical applications. It sets itself apart from sibling methods by explicitly decoupling the rendering parameters (resolution, lighting, sampling) of the context from the focus, rather than relying on a single global metric or hierarchical data structure. The goal is not seamless integration but a tradeoff between achievable quality and speed.
+Focus and Context Multiresolution Rendering is a technique that balances processing capacity between a high-detail "focus" region and a lower-detail "context" region to improve interactivity in time-critical applications. It solves the problem of limited hardware resources in volume rendering by decomposing the scene into two rendering passes with distinct parameters, rather than attempting seamless integration. It sets itself apart from sibling methods like [Level-of-detail (LOD) methods](1007-level-of-detail-lod-methods.md) by using a fixed spatial decomposition (lens) rather than geometric simplification, and from [Dynamic Volume Resolution & Alpha Mask Update](2539-dynamic-volume-resolution-alpha-mask-update.md) by relying on viewport scaling and sampling rate changes rather than data subsampling or alpha masking. See [Focus and Context Multiresolution Rendering](8163-focus-and-context-multiresolution-rendering.md).
 
 ### [Multiresolution Atlas Buffer (MAB)](8219-multiresolution-atlas-buffer-mab.md)
-The Multiresolution Atlas Buffer (MAB) is an acceleration wrapper for Direct Volume Rendering (DVR) in Virtual Reality (VR) that reduces ray density adaptively based on peripheral vision and Head-Mounted Display (HMD) lens distortion, then reconstructs the full-resolution image via compositing. It differs from sibling methods like [Level-of-detail (LOD) methods](1007-level-of-detail-lod-methods.md) or [Dynamic Volume Resolution & Alpha Mask Update](2539-dynamic-volume-resolution-alpha-mask-update.md) by decoupling the raycasting resolution from the final framebuffer resolution through a bidirectional mapping system, rather than modifying the volume data structure or the ray traversal logic itself. Unlike [Focus and Context Multiresolution Rendering](8163-focus-and-context-multiresolution-rendering.md) which may rely on high-speed eye-tracking, MAB assumes a fixed gaze center relative to the HMD lens distortion, making it suitable for standard VR setups without specialized eye-tracking hardware.
+The Multiresolution Atlas Buffer (MAB) is an acceleration wrapper for Direct Volume Rendering (DVR) in Virtual Reality (VR) that reduces ray density adaptively based on peripheral vision and Head-Mounted Display (HMD) lens distortion, then reconstructs the full-resolution image via compositing. It differs from standard foveated rendering by not requiring high-speed eye-tracking hardware; instead, it leverages the fixed nature of HMD lenses and the non-uniform resolution of the human visual system to create a static, gaze-centered resolution map. Unlike sibling methods that modify the ray traversal itself (e.g., [Level-of-detail (LOD) methods](1007-level-of-detail-lod-methods.md)) or dynamic volume resolution ([Dynamic Volume Resolution & Alpha Mask Update](2539-dynamic-volume-resolution-alpha-mask-update.md)), MAB operates as a "bracket" around existing raycasters, modifying ray construction and post-traversal compositing. See [Multiresolution Atlas Buffer (MAB)](8219-multiresolution-atlas-buffer-mab.md).
 
